{"approach":"Start at Barclay Lake trailhead (74920 NE Stevens Pass Hwy, Skykomish, WA 98288). Follow the pleasant level main trail for about 20 minutes to a bridge crossing a large creek (~1 mile). Before the bridge, break right into forest around a large boulder, then walk through relatively open forest or along the creek bed until reaching an open boulder field. Continue through the boulder field to reach steep gullies. Ascend a 60–70° forested rib with fixed lines and natural protection. Climb around the right side of a chossy waterfall (exposed scrambling). Continue up the wash to a ramp with hand lines, then ascend approximately 800 feet of mostly roped-up slab via bolted sections and natural features. Total approach time: 2.5–3.5 hours for competent parties.","approach_variants":[{"baseFinding":"The end of the fixed and roped section is where you unrope and scramble up and left to the base of the tower. The test the first-ascent party wrote down is a negative one and it is the most useful thing on this approach: if what you are doing feels sketchy, you are off the line. The approach is unpleasant but it is protected the whole way — fixed lines, hand lines, bolts — so unprotected, committing ground means you have lost the route, not that the route has got harder. The base of the tower is the foot of a nearly 1,500 ft overhanging prow; there is no mistaking it once you are under it.","distMi":2,"gainFt":3430,"hazards":["Exposed scrambling around the right side of the chossy waterfall","Fixed lines of unknown age and condition","60-70 degree forested rib — a fall here is not survivable ground","Loose rock and brush throughout","Route-finding: the correct line is not obvious even with beta"],"hours":"2.5-4","name":"Barclay Creek Trail, right before the bridge, fixed lines to the tower","notes":"Start at the Barclay Lake trailhead (74920 NE Stevens Pass Hwy, Skykomish). Walk the pleasant, level main Barclay Creek trail for about 20 minutes and roughly a mile to a bridge crossing a large creek — often dry by mid-to-late summer.

Do not cross the bridge. Just before it, break right into the forest around a large boulder. Bushwhack through relatively open forest, or walk the creek bed, until you reach an open boulder field. Cross the boulder field to large trees and an open wash.

The wash leads to a steep, chossy, dirty waterfall; climb around its right side, staying out of the bushes — this stretch is exposed. Above that a ramp with hand lines, then a 60-70 degree forested rib with fixed lines and natural protection, including a low-fifth step in a gully protected by a fixed line, and a small rock face with a bolt. Then roughly 800 ft of mostly roped slab in 35-50 m pitches on bolts and natural gear. Competent parties with good beta take two and a half to four hours; the approach is technical and hard to get right even with information.","season":"Jul-Sep"}]}

